                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#PropRightAnodyne"} (Left/)Right anodyne moprphisms
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cocartesian_fibration_2"} (co)Cartesian fibration
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#categorical_fibration_2"} Categorical fibration
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#inner_fibrations"} Inner fibrations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            For ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            there is the notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
                between two categories.
            %li
              %p
                and the special case of a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+fibered+in+groupoids"} category fibered in groupoids
          %p
            The analog of this for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
            are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibrations"} Cartesian fibrations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+fibrations"}> left fibrations
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+fibrations"} right fibrations
                of quasi-categories.
          %p
            There are more types of fibrations between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            underlying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+fibrations"}> inner fibrations
                – these correspond to
                %strong
                  bundles of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
                \: an inner fibration
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to \Delta[1]
                over the interval is the quasi-categorical analog of a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cograph+of+a+profunctor"} cograph of a profunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C, D
                over the endpoints
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0,1 \in \Delta[1]
                as
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to \Delta[0]
                over the point says precisely that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            %li
              %p
                categorical fibrations – these appear as the fibrations in the sense of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                theory in the Joyal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+quasi-categories"} model structure for quasi-categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi sSet
                        %mi Joyal
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet_{Joyal}
                \. But they have no particular intrinsic meaning in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+Joyal+fibrations"} minimal Joyal fibrations
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p We list the different definitions in the order of their generality. The examples of each definition are also examples of the following definitions.
          %p
            All morphisms in the following are morphisms of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
          %h3#trivial_fibration Trivial fibration
          %p
            A
            %strong trivial fibration
            (trivial
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+lifting+property"} right lifting property
            with respect to the boundary inclusions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Delta[n] \hookrightarrow \Delta[n], n \geq 1
          %h3#kan_fibration Kan fibration
          %ul
            %li
              for the moment see
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
          %p
            A morphism with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+lifting+property"} left lifting property
            against all Kan fibrations is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong anodyne
          %h3#leftright_fibration (Left/)Right fibration
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to S
            is a
            %strong left fibration
            or
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+Kan+fibration"} left Kan fibration
            if it has the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+lifting+property"} right lifting property
            with respect to all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
            inclusions except the right outer ones. It is a
            %strong right fibration
            or
            %strong right Kan fibration
            if its extends against all horns except the left outer ones.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Λ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi n
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mrow
                              %mi k
                              %mo &gt;
                              %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mo ∃
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi S
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Lambda[n]_{k \gt 0} &amp;\to&amp; X
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;{}^{\exists}\nearrow&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\Delta[n] &amp;\to&amp; S
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Morphisms with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+lifting+property"} left lifting property
            against all left/right fibrations are called
            %strong
              left/right
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anodyne+morphism"} anodyne morphism
            maps.
          %p Write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi RFib
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi sSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  RFib(S) \subset sSet/S
          %p
            for the full
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SSet"} SSet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/overcategory"} overcategory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SSet"} SSet
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            on those morphisms that are right fibrations.
          %p
            This is a
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
                of right fibrations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+left+fibrations"} model structure for right fibrations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Grothendieck+construction"} (∞,1)-Grothendieck construction
          %h3#cocartesian_fibration (co)Cartesian fibration
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibration"} Cartesian fibration
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+fibration"} inner fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : X \to S
            such that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for every edge
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %li
              %p
                and every lift
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{y}
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                (that is,
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(\tilde{y})=y
          %p
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+morphism"} Cartesian edge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo :
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f} : \tilde{x} \to \tilde{y}
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            lifting
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %p (HTT, def 2.4.2.1)
          %p see also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+Cartesian+fibrations"} model structure for Cartesian fibrations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Grothendieck+construction"} (∞,1)-Grothendieck construction
          %h3#categorical_fibration Categorical fibration
          %p
            A
            %strong categorical fibration
            is a fibration in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+quasi-categories"} model structure for quasi-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to S
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+lifting+property"} right lifting property
            against all monomorphic
            %em categorical equivalences
            \.
          %p (HTT, p. 81).
          %h3#inner_fibration Inner fibration
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to S
            is an
            %strong inner fibration
            or
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+Kan+fibration"} inner Kan fibration
            if its has the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+lifting+property"} right lifting property
            with respect to all inner
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
            inclusions.
          %p
            The morphisms with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+lifting+property"} left lifting property
            against all inner fibrations are called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong inner anodyne
          %h4#minimal_fibration Minimal fibration
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+Kan+fibration"} minimal Kan fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+Joyal+fibration"} minimal Joyal fibration
          %h2#properties Properties
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              By the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
              we have that every morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              of simplicial sets may be factored as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f : X \to Z \to Y
            %p
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Z
              a left/right/inner anodyne cofibration and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z \to Y
              accordingly a left/right/inner Kan fibration.
          %h3#trivial_fibration_2 Trivial fibration
          %p …
          %h3#kan_fibration_2 Kan fibration
          %p …
          %h3#leftright_fibration_2 (Left/)Right fibration
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Under the operation of forming the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+quasi-category"} opposite quasi-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : C \to D
              is a left fibration then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mi op
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{op} : C^{op} \to D^{op}
              is a right fibration.
            %p Therefore it is sufficient to list properties of only one type of these fibrations, that for the other follows.
          %h4#homotopy_lifting_property Homotopy lifting property
          %p
            In classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : E \to B
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            is said to have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+lifting+property"} homotopy lifting property
            if it has the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+lifting+property"} right lifting property
            with respect to all morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Id
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ×
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \stackrel{(Id, 0)}{\to} Y \times I
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = [0,1]
            the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            and every commuting diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ×
                          %mi I
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Y &amp;\to&amp; E
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y \times I &amp;\to&amp; B
                  }
          %p
            there exists a lift
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ×
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : Y \times I \to E
            making the two triangles
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mi σ
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ×
                          %mi I
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Y &amp;\to&amp; E
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;{}^\sigma\nearrow&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y \times I &amp;\to&amp; B
                  }
          %p
            commute. For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = *
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            this can be rephrased as saying that the universal morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi I
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^I \to B^I \times_B E
            induced by the commuting square commuting square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi E
                            %mi I
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi B
                            %mi I
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  E^I &amp;\to&amp; E
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  B^I &amp;\to&amp; B
                  }
          %p
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            then the lift
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            exists
            %em uniquely
            \. This is the situation that the following proposition generalizes:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : X \to S
              of simplicial sets is a left fibration precisely if the canonical morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X^{\Delta[1]} \to X^{\{0\}} \times_{S^{\{0\}}} S^{\Delta^1}
            %p is a trivial Kan fibration.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              This is a corollary of the characterization of left anodyne morphisms in
              %a{:href => "#PropRightAnodyne"} Properties of left anodyne maps
              by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, corollary 2.1.2.10
          %h4#as_fibrations_in_groupoids
            As fibrations in
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The notion of right fibration of quasi-categories generalizes the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+fibered+in+groupoids"} category fibered in groupoids
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to *
              a right (left) fibration over the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Due to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop. 1.2.5.1
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Right (left) fibrations are preserved by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
          .num_corollary
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              It follows that the fiber
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_c
              of every right fibration
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to C
              over every point
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mi c
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X_c &amp;\to&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\{c\} &amp;\to&amp; C
                    }
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              quasi-categories that are ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              (i.e. simplicial sets that are
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
              is a right fibration precisely if the correspunding ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+fibered+in+groupoids"} category fibered in groupoids
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              This is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop. 2.1.1.3
          %p
            A canonical class of examples of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+category"} fibered category
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bifibration"} bifibration
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-groupoid." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-groupoids." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi K
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : K \to C
              be an arbitrary morphism to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{p/}
              be the corresponding
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+quasi-category"} under quasi-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{p/} \to C
              is a left fibration.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              Due to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop 2.1.2.2
          %h4#PropRightAnodyne (Left/)Right anodyne moprphisms
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_6 Proposition
            %p
              The collection of left anodyne morphisms (those with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+lifting+property"} left lifting property
              against left fibrations) is equivalently
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LAn
                    %mo =
                    %mi LLP
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi RLP
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi LAn
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LAn = LLP(RLP(LAn_0))
              for the following choices of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi LAn
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LAn_0
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi LAn
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LAn_0 =
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the collection of all left
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
                  inclusions
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi Λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mi i
                        %mo →
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi i
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ \Lambda[n]_{i} \to \Delta[n] | 0 \leq i \lt n \}
                  ;
              %li
                %p blah-blah
              %li
                %p blah-blah
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              This is due to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop 2.1.2.6
          %h3#cocartesian_fibration_2 (co)Cartesian fibration
          %h3#categorical_fibration_2 Categorical fibration
          %h3#inner_fibrations Inner fibrations
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_7 Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              of an ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %msub
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi iso
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \simeq_{iso} N(C)
              precisely if the terminal morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \to \Delta[0]
              is an inner fibration with
              %em unique
              inner
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
              fillers, i.e. precisely if for all morphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Lambda[n]_i \to K
            %p
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi i
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt i \lt n
              there is a
              %em unique
              morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n] \to K
              making the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Λ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %msub
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Lambda[n]_i &amp;\to&amp; K
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp; \nearrow
                    \\\
                    \\Delta[n]
                    }
            %p commute.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_6 Proof
            %p
              This is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop. 1.1.2.2
          .num_corollary
            %h6#corollary_2 Corollary
            %p
              It follows that under the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              %em every
              functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : C \to D
              between ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              is an inner fibration.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_7 Proof
            %p This is immediate, but let’s spell it out:
            %p In any commutative diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Λ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mi N
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi D
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Lambda[n] &amp;\to&amp; N(C)
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{N(f)}}
                    \\\
                    \\Delta[n] &amp;&amp; \to&amp; N(D)
                    }
            %p by the above the bottom morphism is already uniquely specified by the remaining diagram.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
